findings.body:
`moveItem` is declared as `moveItem(from, to)`, but the drag handler calls it as `moveItem(newIndex, oldIndex)`:

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="130-145"}

That moves the item you dropped onto back to where the dragged item started, which is the opposite of what the user did.

Example: with `[A, B, C, D]`, drag `A` (index 0) onto `D` (index 3).
- Expected result: `[B, C, D, A]`.
- Actual call: `moveItem(3, 0)`, which gives `[D, A, B, C]`.

::callout{type="warning"}
This is easy to miss in a manual check: swapping two adjacent items gives the same result either way, so a quick one-step drag looks fine. Any drag across two or more slots produces the wrong order and quietly corrupts the form value.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-list.tsx" lines="141-143"}
			if (oldIndex !== -1 && newIndex !== -1) {
				moveItem(oldIndex, newIndex);
			}

The idiomatic alternative is `arrayMove(localKeyedValues, oldIndex, newIndex)` from `@dnd-kit/sortable`.

The PR description lists "Behavioral tests for move up/down menu options" and a "Test verifying prefix items cannot be moved". The diff only adds four `toMatchSnapshot()` cases to `schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx`, and `schema-form.test.tsx` is unchanged.

::diff{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form.snapshot.test.tsx" lines="488-565"}

Snapshots confirm the grip handles render. They can't catch the reversed-argument bug in `handleDragEnd`, because no test ever changes the order. The large generated snapshot, with its `_r_3d_`-style ids and `DndDescribedBy-N` counters, will also churn whenever test order changes.

A good minimum is:
- one test that reorders through the KeyboardSensor (Space, Arrow keys, Space) and checks the resulting `onValuesChange` value;
- one test asserting that prefix items have no `Drag to reorder` button and no Move up/down menu items.

Passing `disabled: !canMove` to `useSortable` disables both dragging and dropping for prefix items. Even though their keys stay in `SortableContext`, a regular item can't be dropped into a tuple slot, and the keyboard coordinate getter skips them. Rendering a `w-5` spacer keeps the grid columns lined up between prefix and regular rows.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/schemas/schema-form-input-array-item.tsx" lines="45-58"}

Reusing the existing `moveItem` for both the menu and the drag path, and keying sortables by the existing stable uuids, is a clean way to integrate.
